WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.984
The permission modes you already use.

2
00:00:02.264 --> 00:00:06.467
You’ve used Claude Code for a year, so you have already lived inside this design.

3
00:00:06.747 --> 00:00:20.123
Its permission docs describe a tiered system: reads within the working directory run without asking, while shell commands (apart from a built-in read-only set), file edits, and web fetches ask in the default mode.

4
00:00:20.403 --> 00:00:31.432
Rules come in three kinds, allow, ask, and deny, written as Tool or Tool(specifier), for example Bash(npm run *) or WebFetch(domain:example.

5
00:00:31.712 --> 00:00:32.437
com).

6
00:00:32.717 --> 00:00:39.971
They are evaluated deny first, then ask, then allow, and the first match wins regardless of how specific a rule is.

7
00:00:40.251 --> 00:00:47.760
So a broad deny such as Bash(aws *) blocks aws s3 ls even if you also allowed that exact command.

8
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:54.611
The docs also note that these rules are enforced by Claude Code, not by the model: instructions in CLAUDE.

9
00:00:54.891 --> 00:00:58.219
md shape what Claude tries, not what it is allowed to do.

10
00:00:58.499 --> 00:01:01.059
The permission modes are presets on that system.

11
00:01:01.339 --> 00:01:12.816
At the time of writing the docs list six: default (reads only run without asking), acceptEdits (adds file edits and common filesystem commands), plan (analyze without editing),

12
00:01:13.096 --> 00:01:29.544
auto (a separate classifier model reviews actions before they run and blocks things like sending sensitive data to external endpoints, production deploys, or force pushes), dontAsk (only pre-approved tools; anything that would prompt is denied, suited to CI),

13
00:01:29.824 --> 00:01:36.032
and bypassPermissions (everything runs, recommended only in isolated containers or VMs).

14
00:01:36.312 --> 00:01:40.984
The docs warn that auto mode reduces prompts but does not guarantee safety.

15
00:01:41.264 --> 00:01:43.568
Read that list as a design catalog.

16
00:01:43.848 --> 00:01:46.621
dontAsk is an allowlist with deny-by-default.

17
00:01:46.901 --> 00:01:53.280
auto replaces human approvals with a model-layer check, which helps with fatigue but is probabilistic.

18
00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:57.763
bypassPermissions is safe only when the environment is the boundary.

19
00:01:58.043 --> 00:02:03.611
The same three choices (rules, human, classifier) appear in every agent you will build.
